        },
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "failures\n",
            "0    294\n",
            "1     40\n",
            "2     12\n",
            "3     11\n",
            "Name: count, dtype: int64\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "x9kLn06uvE4k"
      },
      "source": [
        "**What this shows.**\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Past failures** show a clear pattern: about 79% of students with no past failures passed, against about 60% with one failure. The bars for 2 and 3 failures (about 25% and 36%) have very wide error bars because only 12 and 11 students are in those groups, so their exact values, and the fact that 3 failures is higher than 2, should not be interpreted.\n",
        "- **Study time** has a weak relationship with passing (about 70-74% for categories 1-2 and about 83% for categories 3-4), and the error bars overlap heavily.\n",
        "- **Absences** are strongly right-skewed: most students have few absences and a small number have many.\n",
        "\n",
        "These plots describe associations only. They suggest that `failures` may be an informative feature and `studytime` a weak one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 4. Features, target, and train/test split\n",
        "\n",
        "Features exclude G1, G2, G3 (leakage) and the target `pass`. The split is stratified so train and test keep the same pass rate. All preprocessing happens inside pipelines, so it is learned from training data only."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2xG2P4gQpN-Z"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "X = df_clean.drop(columns=[\"G1\", \"G2\", \"G3\", \"pass\"])\n",
        "y = df_clean[\"pass\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "cat_cols = X.select_dtypes(\"object\").columns.tolist()\n",
        "num_cols = X.select_dtypes(\"number\").columns.tolist()\n",
        "print(\"X shape:\", X.shape, \"|\", len(cat_cols), \"text columns,\", len(num_cols), \"numeric columns\")\n",
        "\n",
        "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n",
        "    X, y, test_size=0.25, stratify=y, random_state=SEED\n",
        ")\n",
        "print(\"Train:\", X_train.shape[0], \"| Test:\", X_test.shape[0])"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "fC-G2b9opJ-8",
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "outputId": "5132b730-011d-40ce-8ec8-7577084f5481"
      },
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "X shape: (357, 30) | 17 text columns, 13 numeric columns\n",
            "Train: 267 | Test: 90\n"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 5. Models"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "IPRK6L5YpfU7"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "def make_logreg():\n",
        "    \"\"\"Logistic Regression: one-hot (drop one level) + scaled numeric columns.\"\"\"\n",
        "    prep = ColumnTransformer([\n",
        "        (\"cat\", OneHotEncoder(drop=\"first\"), cat_cols),\n",
        "        (\"num\", StandardScaler(), num_cols),\n",
        "    ])\n",
        "    return Pipeline([\n",
        "        (\"prep\", prep),\n",
        "        (\"clf\", LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, random_state=SEED)),\n",
        "    ])\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "def make_rf():\n",
        "    \"\"\"Random Forest: one-hot encoding, numeric columns passed through (no scaling needed).\"\"\"\n",
        "    prep = ColumnTransformer(\n",
        "        [(\"cat\", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\"), cat_cols)],\n",
        "        remainder=\"passthrough\",\n",
        "    )\n",
        "    return Pipeline([\n",
        "        (\"prep\", prep),\n",
        "        (\"clf\", RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=300, min_samples_leaf=3, random_state=SEED)),\n",
        "    ])\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "lr_model = make_logreg().fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "rf_model = make_rf().fit(X_train, y_train)"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "R3EVnTx9pWFL"
      },
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 6. Evaluation\n",
        "\n",
        "First a single train/test split (90 test students), then 5-fold cross-validation, which is more reliable on such a small dataset. The baseline predicts \"pass\" for everyone."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "YUzxpxKrphdb"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "def evaluate_on_test(model, name):\n",
        "    pred = model.predict(X_test)\n",
        "    prob = model.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n",
        "    print(f\"=== {name} ===\")\n",
        "    print(classification_report(y_test, pred, target_names=[\"Fail\", \"Pass\"]))\n",
        "    print(\"Accuracy:         \", round(accuracy_score(y_test, pred), 3))\n",
        "    print(\"Balanced accuracy:\", round(balanced_accuracy_score(y_test, pred), 3))\n",
        "    print(\"ROC-AUC:          \", round(roc_auc_score(y_test, prob), 3))\n",
        "    ConfusionMatrixDisplay(confusion_matrix(y_test, pred),\n",
        "                           display_labels=[\"Fail\", \"Pass\"]).plot()\n",
        "    plt.title(f\"{name}: confusion matrix (test set)\")\n",
        "    plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Baseline accuracy on test:\", round(y_test.mean(), 3))\n",
        "evaluate_on_test(lr_model, \"Logistic Regression\")\n",
        "evaluate_on_test(rf_model, \"Random Forest\")"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "iUkBcJz3pm_R",
        "outputId": "ed1b840e-86fe-457e-e79c-94e8fa29865a",
        "colab": {
