    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KyLdr3djvE4p"
      },
      "source": [
        "**What this shows (5-fold cross-validation).**\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Accuracy does not beat the baseline for either model** (about 0.74 for all three, differences smaller than the spread across folds). Accuracy alone would suggest the models learned nothing.\n",
        "- **ROC-AUC and fail recall tell a different story.** Logistic Regression reaches a ROC-AUC of 0.717 and finds about 30% of failing students, while Random Forest reaches 0.669 and finds only about 5%. Both are above the 0.5 of the baseline, so there is some real signal, but it is weak.\n",
        "- **The ranking reversed.** The single split favoured Random Forest, but cross-validation favours Logistic Regression. This shows how unstable comparisons on a small test set are.\n",
        "- The ROC-AUC difference (0.717 vs 0.669) is about the size of the fold-to-fold spread, so on this population the safest conclusion is that Logistic Regression performs at least as well as Random Forest. The extra complexity of the forest did not pay off on this small dataset. Section 8 shows that this ranking does not hold on all 395 students.\n",
        "- Much of the gap in fail recall comes from the default 0.5 decision threshold, not only from what each model learned."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## 7. Explainability"
