    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ZpFwOIgxvE4r"
      },
      "source": [
        "**How to read this.** Positive coefficients increase the predicted chance of passing and negative ones decrease it, holding the other features constant. Numeric features are standardised, and each dummy column is compared with the category that was dropped (for example, `Mjob_services` is compared with `Mjob_at_home`).\n",
        "\n",
        "The largest coefficient is `schoolsup_yes` (extra school support), which is negative. This is unlikely to mean that support harms students. Support is usually given to students who already struggle, so it marks difficulty rather than causing it. `failures` and `goout` are negative, which fits intuition. Some other coefficients, such as `Mjob_teacher` negative and `Fjob_teacher` positive, have no obvious explanation and are likely noise from small groups. These coefficients come from one training split of 267 students, so the next cell checks how stable they are."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.base import clone\n",
        "\n",
        "fold_coefs = []\n",
        "for train_idx, _ in cv.split(X, y):\n",
        "    m = clone(lr_model).fit(X.iloc[train_idx], y.iloc[train_idx])\n",
        "    names = m.named_steps[\"prep\"].get_feature_names_out()\n",
        "    fold_coefs.append(pd.Series(m.named_steps[\"clf\"].coef_[0], index=names))\n",
        "\n",
        "coef_cv = pd.DataFrame(fold_coefs)\n",
        "stability = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"mean\": coef_cv.mean(),\n",
        "    \"std\": coef_cv.std(),\n",
        "    \"same_sign_folds\": (np.sign(coef_cv) == np.sign(coef_cv.mean())).sum(),\n",
        "})\n",
        "stability[\"abs_mean\"] = stability[\"mean\"].abs()\n",
        "stability.sort_values(\"abs_mean\", ascending=False).head(10).drop(columns=\"abs_mean\").round(3)"
      ],
